threads.detector:
Outcome-blind. It runs on blocks (detectors/scales.py:222) after the codes are removed (survey-sentinel-codes). Before any key is declared, it shows each item's corrected item–rest correlation (polyserial preferred; Pearson labelled as attenuated toward zero) and its general-factor loading, in questionnaire order. Nothing is pre-selected. No reversal is proposed. There is no 'ω if flipped' preview and no pass or fail at 0.30. A negative value has four explanations, and the values cannot tell them apart:
- the item needs reversing;
- it was already reversed upstream (a REDCap calculated field or a codebook note). An '_r' suffix is ambiguous, because it is also a retest suffix (core/scales.py:61);
- it loads on a wording factor (survey-wording-method-factor);
- it does not belong.

After the key is declared, the same values and α and ω-total are shown under the key, as a consequence and never as evidence. An item still negative under the key means the key was applied twice or the item does not belong (stages/scales.py:309). Measured on survey_instrument.csv:
- the 8 keyed items are raw −0.357 to −0.647;
- under the codebook's key, α goes from 0.796 to 0.942 and ω-total is 0.943 (engine omega(), complete rows);
- the smallest keyed item–rest r is 0.38 on complete cases (0.35 pairwise).

When the scale is the outcome, the same view counts as outcome-alone.

Outcome-blind. The block's response run is the longest run of consecutive whole numbers in the union of every item's values, never read item by item (detectors/scales.py:317). A value separated from the run by a gap of at least 2 is a code when it is conventional (7/8/9, 77/88/99, 98, 999, −1/−8/−9) or lies at least 3 steps past the run. Flagged items are ranked by the shift in the item mean between counting codes as answers and excluding them. The test rejects a scale that really is wider: a 0–10 rating has no gap. Single NHANES-style columns (7/9, 77/99, 7777/9999) go through the per-column code reader (detectors/codes.py:59, :192). Censoring codes belong to survey-top-codes. Measured on survey_sentinels.csv:
- 102 codes in 5 items;
- item_14 has 33 nines (11.0%), and its mean is 3.70 with them and 3.05 without (shift 0.65);
- item_05, a reversed item, has item–rest r of −0.37 with its 9s and −0.58 without.

Runs after the codes are recoded. For each item it measures:
- the don't-know and refused rates;
- whether they concentrate in knowledge items or sensitive items (income, alcohol, weight);
- how well a 'said don't know' indicator is predicted from the predictors only (age, sex, education, mode), reported as a cross-validated AUC with its CI. The outcome is never read.

A patterned rate rejects MCAR only. It does not reject MAR given those covariates, and it says nothing about whether 'don't know' is a substantive answer, which the item's content decides. Its job is to name the covariates the imputation model must condition on. Fixture survey_sentinels.csv is a negative control, measured in session: item_22's 'don't know' rate is 7.5% (high school), 7.1% (some college), 6.8% (bachelor's) and 10.8% (graduate), so it is not concentrated among the less educated.

Outcome-blind. Two signals:
- repdigit values (5…5, 6…6) isolated above the column's support by a gap. Without a codebook these are read only in an NHANES-like table (detectors/codes.py _nhanes_like), so a genuine 5555 in a kJ or step-count column is never flagged;
- a spike at the column's maximum that holds far more than its neighbour (RIDAGEYR = 80; INDFMPIR = 5.00).

The codebook's value table settles which is which (§14.2; codebook.py:748); otherwise the user is asked. Alternatives to reject:
- a genuine extreme (no gap, no spike);
- a missing code such as 7777/9999 (survey-sentinel-codes);
- a heap at a natural bounded maximum, such as 7 days a week or 100% (detectors/codes.py:29). The spike signal fires on these too, so a spike at a natural bound is always asked, never stated.

Outcome-blind, per scale:
- each respondent's completion count, and the rows complete cases would lose;
- per-item skip rates; an item skipped at least 3× more than the median item (a self-harm item, income) is surfaced and routed to survey-dont-know-meaning;
- section nonresponse (every item blank), separated from item nonresponse;
- monotone blanks running to the end of the questionnaire, which go to survey-breakoff.

Eekhout's thresholds are context and still need checking: the engine quotes only the 25% one. Measured on survey_instrument.csv: 36 respondents each skipped exactly one item (12 each in item_07, item_19 and item_31), so complete cases would lose 36 of 300 rows (12%). There are no break-offs.

Outcome-blind. It needs the questionnaire's order from the codebook or a module map. Export column order is often alphabetical or merged by module, so it is not evidence. Given that order, it measures:
- the missing share by position;
- per respondent, a monotone pattern (once blank, every later item blank), whose first blank is the break-off point;
- break-off points clustered at one page or module;
- how break-off respondents differed on the early items they did answer.

Three alternatives also produce monotone blanks:
- a skip gate (survey-skip-pattern-blanks);
- a module given to a subsample, where a weight is positive exactly where it is asked (survey-subsample-weight);
- a module-file merge, where the respondent's ID is absent from that component file.

Outcome-blind: P(blank in B | gate) ≈ 1 and P(blank | not gate) ≈ 0. The gate can take three forms:
- a single answer (ALQ111 'No' skips ALQ130; BPQ020/BPQ080 'No' skips the medication items);
- an OR of conditions within a module (ALQ130 is also blank for past-year abstainers);
- an age universe (blank if and only if age < k, as with items asked only of adults 18 or 20 and over).

These are found by testing the module's candidate gates, their disjunctions and age thresholds. When the codebook's skip instructions are loaded, they settle the gates. A skip gate and a missingness flag produce the same values, so the role:flag reading asks (readings.py:1128; §14.3 amendment). Alternatives:
- MAR missingness: blanks not tied to any gate;
- a missingness flag;
- a subsample module, where a weight is positive exactly where the item is asked (survey-subsample-weight).

Outcome-blind. The signature:
- three or more columns sharing a stem (detectors/scales.py:122 _stem), each holding a single code (its own code is allowed: SMQ690A–H hold 1, 2, 3…) with blanks, and never a 0;
- per respondent, the count ticked;
- rows with nothing ticked anywhere in the block are the only candidates for item nonresponse. A 'none of these' column, when present, separates 'none' from 'skipped'.

Alternatives: a set of yes/no items with real missing values (they hold 0s), and a skip-gated block, where a gate column predicts the all-blank rows. The latter goes to survey-skip-pattern-blanks.

Per respondent, from their own answers only (the outcome is never read):
- longstring (the longest run of identical consecutive answers);
- intra-individual response variability;
- even–odd consistency and psychometric synonyms;
- Mahalanobis D²;
- raw agreement with both an item and its reverse-worded twin, a test on the raw answers before keying;
- duplicate response vectors;
- completion time and attention-check items, when they exist.

The alternatives to reject are a real floor respondent (a well person answering 0 to every PHQ item) and a genuinely neutral respondent. Midpoint straightlining survives reversal (6 − 3 = 3), so reversed pairs cannot flag it. Only completion time, attention checks or D² can separate midpoint inattention from neutrality, and when none exists the canvas says the values cannot settle it. The flagged share is reported with Curran's thresholds. Fixture survey_instrument.csv is a negative control, measured: the longest longstring is 11 of 40, and no row has zero variance. Nothing is shown when nothing fires.

Any of:
- a repeat administration's columns (same stem with _t2 or _retest; scales.py:61 RETEST_SUFFIXES, :633) or its score, checked to fit the score (:540). The guesser needs every item to carry the suffix, so collisions with '_2' or '_r' are unlikely;
- a reference measure blank outside a subsample, read as an amount (:579; calibrate_reference);
- self-reported and measured versions of one quantity in the same table (NHANES WHD010/WHD020 beside BMXHT/BMXWT). Their agreement is shown as Bland–Altman bias and limits, and whether the bias differs by sex, age or BMI, from predictors only.

Outcome-blind. The lead signal is bootstrap stability:
- the share of resamples keeping the same number of factors under parallel analysis;
- Tucker's congruence (φ) of the loadings;
- the bootstrap CI of ω.

n against items (n < 100, fewer than 5 per item) and n per group for DIF (about 100–200) are context only. MacCallum 1999 and Mundfrom 2005 show that the n needed depends on communalities and overdetermination. The fixture passes (300 rows for 40 items).

The block is fingerprinted by stem, item count and response scale against the instrument library (detectors/scales.py:72 INSTRUMENTS: PHQ-9, PHQ-8, GAD-7, HADS, CES-D, RSES, PSS, EQ-5D). NHANES stems must be added: the PHQ-9 ships as DPQ010–DPQ090, with DPQ100 a separate difficulty item outside the score. Verified in session: the PHQ-9 pattern is ^phq(?:_?9)?$, so 'dpq' matches nothing.

A match is always a hypothesis (§B1.1), read in three grades:
- full: stem, count and scale all match;
- offset: a 1–4 export of a 0–3 instrument, read as a recode to subtract, not a modified instrument;
- partial: 8 of 9 PHQ items, read as 'modified, or not this instrument'.

Outcome-blind, per self-reported amount. The share at multiples of 5 and 10 (and at 7, 14 and 30 for day counts, 0.5 for hours) is compared with a smoothed expectation, giving an observed/expected ratio per heap. Whipple's and Myers' age-heaping indices are analogues, not the measure. Heaping that differs by group (age, education, proxy, mode) is flagged. Alternatives:
- real mass: packs hold 20, so 20 a day is partly real;
- a scale end (detectors/codes.py:29);
- codes, which sit apart behind a gap (survey-sentinel-codes, survey-top-codes).

It takes a confirmed scale (from the keying, dimensionality and reliability threads) and measures:
- its item count k against n or the number of events;
- pairwise item correlations (near-duplicates at r > 0.8; α above Terwee's 0.95 signals redundancy);
- the importance dilution expected among correlated features.

Measured by the critic on survey_instrument.csv: 40 items on 300 rows, keyed α 0.94.

Outcome-blind:
- item columns declared in two scales, or in a predictor scale and the outcome scale;
- an outcome computed from a column that is also a predictor;
- name and label overlap, which is a proposal only; the user confirms which items are the same question.

Outcome-blind:
- Kish's effective n = (Σw)²/Σw²;
- the weighting design effect 1 + CV²(w), labelled as exact for means only. For regression coefficients the weighting deff depends on how the weights correlate with the variables (Spencer 2000);
- the share of total weight held by the top 1% of rows;
- max/median weight.

For a rare yes/no outcome it also computes the weighted effective number of events, which counts the outcome only and so is an outcome-alone view.

A rule that follows from the settled population answer:
- the weighted and unweighted distribution of each explained input (SMD) is shown at any time; it is outcome-blind;
- as a model view (after the lock under inference, recorded under prediction), mean |SHAP|, permutation importance and ALE are computed with the design weights;
- the SHAP background (reference) distribution is weighted too, since otherwise attributions describe deviations from the sample's mean prediction;
- the rank change against the unweighted summary is recorded.

Outcome-blind, with a settled cycle or wave column:
- per item, the distribution by cycle;
- a step at one cycle boundary larger than the drift between adjacent cycles (SMD ≥ 0.2, a category that appears or vanishes, or a changed response run read by the block reader);
- a variable present only in some cycles;
- codebook value tables that differ by cycle (§14.2);
- the codebook's variable lineage, so a renamed item (ALQ120Q → ALQ121) is checked once the user pools it.

Alternatives: real secular change (a steady trend with no step), and changed composition (the step persists after reweighting for age and sex).

Outcome-blind:
- the number of candidate exposure columns beyond a declared hypothesis set (more than about 20), grouped by questionnaire module (DBQ, PAQ, SMQ, ALQ…);
- their correlation structure (median |r|, clusters by module);
- with pooled cycles, how many independent cycles exist for a discovery/replication split.

With a settled subject identifier and wave column, it reads:
- retention per wave, and monotone against intermittent patterns;
- standardized differences at baseline between stayers and leavers on predictors (outcome-blind);
- when the outcome is also measured at baseline, the stayer–leaver difference on it. This is the most telling view, and as an outcome-alone view it may be shown;
- a longitudinal weight positive only for continuing respondents.

Alternative: planned rotation, where a rotating panel retires a cohort by design. The rotation-group column or the codebook separates it. Built once in the repeats lane for every lens; the survey lens adds the longitudinal weight.

It needs a settled subject identifier with 5 or more administrations per person and time stamps (the repeats reading). Per item and scale, it reads:
- the ICC (the between-person share of variance);
- multilevel reliability at each level (ω-within, ω-between), the survey add-on;
- the within-person SD of each time-varying exposure.

Outcome-blind. Built once in the repeats lane for every lens.

Outcome-blind first. The questionnaire's routing (the skip-gate reading) and the codebook labels link items to the declared outcome's condition:
- diagnosis items (DIQ010 'ever told you had diabetes');
- treatment items (DIQ050 insulin; DIQ070 pills; BPQ050A);
- advice items (MCQ366a–d);
- age-at-diagnosis items (DID040);
- every follow-up gated on these, including their missingness indicators.

Value leaks and blank-pattern leaks are told apart by each item's universe. DIQ050 is asked of everyone, so its value leaks but its blank does not. DID040 and DIQ070 are gated on DIQ010/DIQ160, so their blanks encode the diagnosis. These universes are to be verified against the DIQ documentation.

The concept link is a proposal from labels and routing and is asked; names never settle it (§14.3). Under prediction, a confirming predictor–outcome check is recorded: any single input, or its missingness indicator, whose discrimination alone exceeds AUC 0.90 is flagged for the question. The alternative to reject is a strong, legitimate predictor such as measured waist for diabetes: it is neither gated on nor worded about the outcome's condition, so it is not proposed.

Outcome-blind. It needs three things:
- one interview per person (repeats settled as a single occasion);
- a current-behavior exposure (diet, alcohol, activity, supplements);
- awareness or advice items about the outcome's condition or its precursors: MCQ366a–d 'told to control weight, increase exercise, reduce salt, reduce fat'; MCQ371a–d 'are you now doing'; DIQ010; DIQ160 (prediabetes).

The share told or advised is shown at any time, as a single-variable view. The exposure among the advised against the not-advised is held until the lock under inference, and recorded under prediction, whenever the advice concerns the outcome's condition: the advice is then a near-proxy for the outcome, so the comparison previews the exposure–outcome direction. When the advice concerns an unrelated condition, it is a predictors-only view.

Outcome-blind. It needs a lifetime (ever) item and a current item for the same behavior. Examples:
- alcohol: NHANES ALQ111 (from 2017–18), or ALQ101/ALQ110 ('12 or more drinks' in a year or a lifetime) in earlier cycles, with past-12-month frequency;
- smoking: SMQ020 (100 cigarettes in life) with SMQ040 (now smoke);
- supplements: ever against the past 30 days.

The ever item differs by cycle, so the never/former split is harmonized across pooled cycles first (survey-instrument-changed-across-cycles). When the analyzed exposure is current use, it reports the share of current non-users who are former users, and how former users differ from never users on predictors (age, number of conditions, and self-rated health HSD010 only when it is neither the outcome nor a mediator). The alternative is that no lifetime item exists, so the zero group cannot be split; that becomes a stated limitation, never a guess.

Outcome-blind. Each item's reference period is read from the codebook's question text ('in the past 30 days', 'past 12 months', 'in a typical week', 'usually', 'ever', 'now'). Without a codebook it is read from value ranges that imply a window (0–30 day counts, 0–7 days per week, 0–365). It flags:
- a derived quantity that combines items with different windows;
- an exposure whose window postdates the outcome's, or differs from it (current supplement use against 'ever told');
- items pooled across cycles whose window changed, which goes to survey-instrument-changed-across-cycles.

The alternative to reject is a combination the instrument itself publishes (AUDIT-C, an NHANES derived variable), which the instrument library or codebook names.
